<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🌙 LIVE ATMOSPHERIC VISIBILITY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 6, 2026 11:03AM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 6, 2026 12:03PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE ATMOSPHERIC VISIBILITY</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (analysis), hourly cycles</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/visibility/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261006-t14z-vis-anl-g2" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Meteorological visibility in statute miles (HRRR surface analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> maroon obscured &rarr; red &rarr; orange &rarr; yellow moderate &rarr; green good &rarr; cyan &rarr; blue excellent &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 30</b> deep-blue crystal clear. Same visibility always shows the same color; above 30 mi clamps into deep blue. Human-observer/mariner/pilot visibility through haze, mist, precipitation, and smoke — not astronomical seeing.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km surface visibility analysis, hourly cycles<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE ATMOSPHERIC VISIBILITY</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 6, 2026 11:03AM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 6, 2026 12:03PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE ATMOSPHERIC VISIBILITY</h2><p>Near-surface meteorological visibility from the operational HRRR analysis: how far a human observer, mariner, or pilot can distinguish surroundings through haze, mist, precipitation, and smoke. Low values flag navigation and shoreline hazards; high values mark crystal-clear Great Lakes air.</p><p>Visibility science (atmospheric optics and observing practice) quantifies horizontal contrast loss from scattering and absorption by hydrometeors and aerosols. It underpins aviation flight rules, marine navigation advisories, and air-quality perception, and is reported operationally in statute miles across the United States.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/visibility/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261006-t14z-vis-anl-g2" width="600" alt="key"><br>Meteorological visibility in statute miles (HRRR surface analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> maroon obscured &rarr; red &rarr; orange &rarr; yellow moderate &rarr; green good &rarr; cyan &rarr; blue excellent &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 30</b> deep-blue crystal clear. Same visibility always shows the same color; above 30 mi clamps into deep blue. Human-observer/mariner/pilot visibility through haze, mist, precipitation, and smoke — not astronomical seeing.<br><b>Units:</b> mi, statute (display; source m / 1609.344)<br><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km surface visibility analysis, hourly cycles<br><b>Update:</b> Checked hourly; republishes only on a newer HRRR cycle.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p>]]></description>
